DISCLOSURE
==========

What this package does that could look suspicious to an automated scanner,
and why each of those behaviours exists.

1. NETWORK EGRESS TO MANY HOSTS
   web-search, fetch-page and deep-research make outbound HTTP requests to a
   large number of third-party hosts: search engines, public APIs (Hacker News,
   StackExchange, GitHub, arXiv, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, Crossref, Wikipedia),
   and general-purpose CORS relays (api.allorigins.win, api.cors.lol,
   api.codetabs.com, r.jina.ai). The relays exist because DuckDuckGo and Bing
   refuse datacenter IPs; the code tries them in order and caches answers
   locally. This is a research/reading tool: it makes the same requests a
   human would make with a browser.

2. PROXY / RELAY ENDPOINTS
   The relay list is a plain array of public CORS proxies, used to fetch a URL
   the local IP cannot reach. It is not a tunnel, it does not hide the user
   from the destination, and there is no credential handling of any kind.

3. BASE64 DECODING OF REDIRECT PARAMETERS
   Search engines wrap result links (duckduckgo.com/l/?uddg=…, bing.com/ck/a?u=a1<base64>).
   The code decodes those wrappers to recover the real URL. It is standard
   SERP parsing, not obfuscation of anything the package itself sends.

4. NO INSTALL-TIME BEHAVIOUR
   There are no postinstall/preinstall scripts, no npm lifecycle hooks, no
   binaries, no native builds. Installing the package copies Python files. The
   only command that downloads anything is install.sh, which a human runs
   deliberately to fetch ffmpeg/piper and which prints every step.

5. SUBPROCESS USE
   ffmpeg/ffprobe are invoked with fixed, locally-built argument vectors for
   media conversion. No shell=True, no user-controlled command strings.

6. NO CREDENTIALS, EVER
   The package never asks for, stores or transmits an account, token, cookie or
   password. If a page needs a login, it says so and stops.

Nothing here is offensive tooling. It is a media toolkit plus a web reader.

